Context: Chapter III of the 1986 notes builds 'réseaux' (networks or maps on surfaces) from 'découpages' (dissections) inside a tame ('modérée') topology. On p.32 Grothendieck axiomatizes an ordered set M of admissible subsets. It has a smallest element ∅ and a largest element 1, and a symmetric relation '{X,Y} modulaire' that holds when X⊂Y. For modular pairs, ∧ and ∨ exist. Note ∨ is not the set union. Axiom 4 is a complement: X' is the largest Y with {X,Y} modular and X∧Y=∅. The map X↦X' is an order-reversing involution satisfying De Morgan on modular pairs. M_f is the subset of 'closed' elements, and there is a closure X↦X̄ into M_f, compatible with ∨ on modular pairs (axioms 5–7).

This page:
(8) Since {X,X'} is modular, so is {X̄, X̄'}. The frontier is defined as Fr X := X̄ ∧ X̄'. By De Morgan and the duality closure/interior, (Fr X)' = X° ∨ X'°, where X° = (X̄')' is the interior. Then: if {X,Y} is modular, {Fr X, Fr Y} is modular, so Fr X ∨ Fr Y is defined (and is again admissible).

NB: in the concrete case (tame subsets of a surface, with L presumably the admissible curves or arcs), several conditions on X ∈ M are compared:
- (a) X ⊂ X^•, i.e. X° = ∅, i.e. X̄' = 1, i.e. X' is dense;
- (b) X = X^•, i.e. X is closed with empty interior, i.e. X' is open dense;
- (c) X is covered by finitely many pieces of L.
The point is that 'thin' tame sets are exactly the finite unions of pieces of curves.

Second part: take an ordered set M satisfying 1)–7), with 4) strengthened as indicated, presumably so that complementation and modularity behave globally. Then M becomes a Boolean ring:
- X+Y = (X∨Y)∧(X∧Y)' (symmetric difference);
- X·Y = X∧Y;
- 0 = ∅ and 1 = the top element;
- conversely X∨Y = X+Y−XY, X∧Y = XY, and X≤Y ⇔ XY = X.
This is the Stone correspondence between Boolean algebras and Boolean rings, now with a closure operator: a closure algebra in the sense of McKinsey–Tarski.

Next page (34): the axioms are relativized to the lower set M_{≤A}, with relative complement X'∧A. This prepares an inductive or local treatment of dissections.
